<article class="env-lemma" id="053M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/053M">Lemma <span data-tag="053M">29.21.4</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes which is locally of finite type. Let $g : S' \to S$ be any morphism. Denote $f' : X' \to S'$ the base change. If $x' \in X'$ maps to a point $x \in X$ which is closed in $X_{f(x)}$ then $x'$ is closed in $X'_{f'(x')}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The residue field $\kappa (x')$ is a quotient of $\kappa (f'(x')) \otimes _{\kappa (f(x))} \kappa (x)$, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01JT" data-tag="01JT">26.17.5</a>. Hence it is a finite extension of $\kappa (f'(x'))$ as $\kappa (x)$ is a finite extension of $\kappa (f(x))$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/01TF" data-tag="01TF">29.21.3</a>. Thus we see that $x'$ is closed in its fibre by applying that lemma one more time. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>